    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(36, 69), (58, 77), (86, 33), (41, 49), (96, 87)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 41.0, 44.0, 32.0]\n    generator_capacities = [194.0, 156.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 500.0  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 220.0  # kV\n    line_capacity = 100.0  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 100000.0  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 50.0  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 five\u2011node transmission buildout with quadratic loss costs\n\nWe have been asked to plan a cost\u2011effective transmission buildout that connects two generator sites to three consumer sites across five total nodes. The planning area and node locations are provided as map coordinates in Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (36, 69)\n  - N1 (generator): (58, 77)\n  - N2 (consumer): (86, 33)\n  - N3 (consumer): (41, 49)\n  - N4 (consumer): (96, 87), and the role of each site (generator or consumer) is given in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']. The corresponding demand requirements are listed in Consumer demands: N2: 41.0 MW, N3: 44.0 MW, N4: 32.0 MW, and the maximum available output at the generator sites is provided in Generator capacities: N0: 194.0 MW, N1: 156.0 MW.\n\nThere are no preset corridors; we may propose overhead line segments between any pair of nodes. A line, once built, carries power in either direction, but only up to its thermal rating, which is specified by 100.0 MW. Construction cost scales with the straight\u2011line route length between endpoints at the rate in $100,000/km.\n\nThe conductors will be copper. Resistive heating is therefore a primary operational cost driver, and it grows rapidly with loading. The material property for copper is given in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), and the selected conductor size is 500.0 mm\u00b2. For a given span, the electrical resistance increases with distance and decreases with cross\u2011sectional area, so longer and smaller conductors are more expensive to operate. We will run the network at the voltage level in 220.0 kV; higher voltage directly reduces current for a given power transfer, which cuts resistive heating substantially because of the square law. Specifically, the heating cost on a line tracks the physical relationship that losses scale with the resistance times the square of power divided by voltage (Loss ~ Resistance \u00d7 (Power/Voltage)^2). Doubling the power flow makes the resistive losses roughly four times larger, which is precisely why the chosen voltage level is so important.\n\nFor planning purposes, we will treat resistive losses as a financial penalty rather than subtracting them from the nodal balances. In other words, power is conserved at each node: generator outputs and line inflows equal line outflows and served demand at the nodes, and any losses are accounted for separately as a cost. The monetary value of these heating losses should be charged at the rate in $50.0/MWh. This approach lets us quantify the trade\u2011off between building more or longer lines (capital cost) versus operating them harder (quadratic heating costs).\n\nKey planning requirements:\n- We can only move power along lines we choose to build.\n- Each consumer node must receive at least its required demand from Consumer demands: N2: 41.0 MW, N3: 44.0 MW, N4: 32.0 MW; delivering surplus is acceptable.\n- Generator outputs cannot exceed their limits in Generator capacities: N0: 194.0 MW, N1: 156.0 MW.\n- Each built line is bidirectional but cannot exceed its rating from 100.0 MW in either direction.\n- Construction cost for each candidate line equals its length multiplied by $100,000/km.\n- Operational cost for each line follows the square\u2011law: more loading causes disproportionately higher heating costs, valued at $50.0/MWh, with losses scaling with resistance based on 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), line length, and 500.0 mm\u00b2, and decreasing with the square of 220.0 kV.\n\nThe objective is to identify which lines to build and how to route power so that all consumer demands are met, generator limits are respected, and the total long\u2011run cost is minimized. That total cost is the sum of:\n- Construction expenditures dictated by distances and $100,000/km.\n- Ongoing heating costs that reflect the true physics of resistive losses (not a linear proxy), increasing with the square of line loading and reduced by operating at higher voltage.\n\nIn short, the model must explicitly capture the square\u2011law nature of resistive losses and the 1/V^2 benefit of running at higher voltage, while enforcing capacity limits and ensuring service to all customers. This will allow us to make transparent trade\u2011offs between building additional capacity and paying for higher quadratic operating costs over the network defined by Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (36, 69)\n  - N1 (generator): (58, 77)\n  - N2 (consumer): (86, 33)\n  - N3 (consumer): (41, 49)\n  - N4 (consumer): (96, 87), ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer'], Consumer demands: N2: 41.0 MW, N3: 44.0 MW, N4: 32.0 MW, Generator capacities: N0: 194.0 MW, N1: 156.0 MW, 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), 500.0 mm\u00b2, 220.0 kV, 100.0 MW, $100,000/km, and $50.0/MWh.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 five\u2011node transmission buildout with quadratic loss costs\n\nWe have been asked to plan a cost\u2011effective transmission buildout that connects two generator sites to three consumer sites across five total nodes. The planning area and node locations are provided as map coordinates in {NODE_COORDINATES}, and the role of each site (generator or consumer) is given in {NODE_TYPES}. The corresponding demand requirements are listed in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and the maximum available output at the generator sites is provided in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.\n\nThere are no preset corridors; we may propose overhead line segments between any pair of nodes. A line, once built, carries power in either direction, but only up to its thermal rating, which is specified by {LINE_CAPACITY}. Construction cost scales with the straight\u2011line route length between endpoints at the rate in {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}.\n\nThe conductors will be copper. Resistive heating is therefore a primary operational cost driver, and it grows rapidly with loading. The material property for copper is given in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the selected conductor size is {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. For a given span, the electrical resistance increases with distance and decreases with cross\u2011sectional area, so longer and smaller conductors are more expensive to operate. We will run the network at the voltage level in {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}; higher voltage directly reduces current for a given power transfer, which cuts resistive heating substantially because of the square law. Specifically, the heating cost on a line tracks the physical relationship that losses scale with the resistance times the square of power divided by voltage (Loss ~ Resistance \u00d7 (Power/Voltage)^2). Doubling the power flow makes the resistive losses roughly four times larger, which is precisely why the chosen voltage level is so important.\n\nFor planning purposes, we will treat resistive losses as a financial penalty rather than subtracting them from the nodal balances. In other words, power is conserved at each node: generator outputs and line inflows equal line outflows and served demand at the nodes, and any losses are accounted for separately as a cost. The monetary value of these heating losses should be charged at the rate in {LOSS_COST_RATE}. This approach lets us quantify the trade\u2011off between building more or longer lines (capital cost) versus operating them harder (quadratic heating costs).\n\nKey planning requirements:\n- We can only move power along lines we choose to build.\n- Each consumer node must receive at least its required demand from {POWER_DEMANDS}; delivering surplus is acceptable.\n- Generator outputs cannot exceed their limits in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.\n- Each built line is bidirectional but cannot exceed its rating from {LINE_CAPACITY} in either direction.\n- Construction cost for each candidate line equals its length multiplied by {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}.\n- Operational cost for each line follows the square\u2011law: more loading causes disproportionately higher heating costs, valued at {LOSS_COST_RATE}, with losses scaling with resistance based on {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, line length, and {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}, and decreasing with the square of {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}.\n\nThe objective is to identify which lines to build and how to route power so that all consumer demands are met, generator limits are respected, and the total long\u2011run cost is minimized. That total cost is the sum of:\n- Construction expenditures dictated by distances and {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}.\n- Ongoing heating costs that reflect the true physics of resistive losses (not a linear proxy), increasing with the square of line loading and reduced by operating at higher voltage.\n\nIn short, the model must explicitly capture the square\u2011law nature of resistive losses and the 1/V^2 benefit of running at higher voltage, while enforcing capacity limits and ensuring service to all customers. This will allow us to make transparent trade\u2011offs between building additional capacity and paying for higher quadratic operating costs over the network defined by {NODE_COORDINATES}, {NODE_TYPES}, {POWER_DEMANDS}, {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}, {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}, {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}, {LINE_CAPACITY}, {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}, and {LOSS_COST_RATE}."
